  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Given name then family name; most people have one or two middle names too. Many Finnish surnames end in -nen (Korhonen, Virtanen) or come from nature and places (Niemi, Laine, Mäki). Finland-Swedes, mostly on the coast and in Helsinki, carry Swedish given names and surnames such as Lindholm or Nyström. Russian-speaking Finns use Finnish transliteration (Jelena, Mihail), and women's surnames take -ova or -eva. People use first names and the informal 'sinä' almost everywhere.",
